From 5a35d95ec4f995f2d8720d56131f0b37aabd9f9c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 21:46:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [NAD] Add configurable geometry coordinate precision in the
 SVG output

Diagram geometry coordinates are written with a hard-coded 2 decimal places, on
coordinates that are typically in the thousands (e.g. translate(-5372.65,...)).
Once the viewBox is scaled to the screen, that is sub-pixel precision, so the
extra decimals are wasted bytes: on a large network the NAD SVG is tens of MB,
and the client viewer's first paint is dominated by parsing + styling + laying
out that DOM (measured ~44% parse / ~32% style / ~24% layout, all scaling with
output size).

Add an `SvgParameters.geometryPrecision` option (default 2, reproducing the
previous output) that controls the number of decimals used by every geometry
coordinate (positions, radii, paths, transforms). Lowering it shrinks the SVG
(smaller transfer + faster client parsing) at the cost of geometric precision.

- SvgWriter.getFormattedValue now uses the configured precision (and the loop
  path string, previously a hard-coded %.2f, goes through it too).
- The parameter is excluded from the diagram metadata serialization
  (@JsonIgnoreProperties): it is a generation-side output knob the viewer does
  not consume, so reference metadata and all existing SVG references are
  unchanged and the full NAD test suite passes at the default.
- New GeometryPrecisionTest verifies precision 0 < 2 < 3 in output size and that
  precision 0 yields integer coordinates.

Note: bus-annulus arc paths still use a fixed 3-decimal format; routing those
through the parameter too is left as a follow-up.
